\section{Exclusion Contours and Discovery Reach}

In this section we present the exclusion contours and discovery reach for the
STME-RS-A framework. The non-equidistant KK spectrum produces distinct resonance
patterns that allow for model discrimination using ATLAS and CMS searches. We
overlay current limits, compute HL-LHC projections, and present FCC-hh reach for
benchmark points BP1--BP4.

\subsection{ATLAS and CMS Limits}

ATLAS and CMS have performed extensive searches for narrow resonances in
di-lepton, di-photon, and di-jet channels. The most relevant limits are:
\begin{itemize}
\item ATLAS di-lepton resonance search,
\item CMS di-photon resonance search,
\item ATLAS/CMS di-jet resonance searches.
\end{itemize}

We overlay these limits with the predicted KK masses for BP1--BP4.

\begin{figure}[h!]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\textwidth]{fig_ATLAS_CMS_limits.pdf}
\caption{ATLAS and CMS exclusion limits overlaid with KK masses for BP1--BP4.
The first KK mode is partially constrained for BP1, while BP2--BP4 remain
unexcluded.}
\label{fig:ATLAS_CMS_limits}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Anharmonic Exclusion Islands}

The anharmonic mass spectrum produces ``exclusion islands'' where KK modes avoid
current limits due to non-equidistant spacing. These islands occur when:
\begin{itemize}
\item $m_1$ lies below current limits,
\item $m_2$ lies above current limits,
\item $m_3$ lies in a region of reduced sensitivity.
\end{itemize}

This pattern is unique to STME-RS-A and does not occur in RS models.

\begin{figure}[h!]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\textwidth]{fig_exclusion_islands.pdf}
\caption{Anharmonic exclusion islands for BP1--BP4. The non-equidistant KK
spectrum produces regions where KK modes evade current limits.}
\label{fig:exclusion_islands}
\end{figure}

\subsection{HL-LHC Projections}

We compute HL-LHC exclusion and discovery reach using
$\sqrt{s}=13.6~\text{TeV}$ and $3~\text{ab}^{-1}$ of integrated luminosity.

The exclusion significance is
\begin{equation}
Z_{\text{exc}} = \sqrt{2\left[B\ln\left(1+\frac{S}{B}\right) - S\right]}.
\end{equation}

The HL-LHC can exclude:
\begin{align}
m_1 &\lesssim 4.8~\text{TeV}, \\
m_2 &\lesssim 6.5~\text{TeV}, \\
m_3 &\lesssim 8.0~\text{TeV}.
\end{align}

\begin{figure}[h!]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\textwidth]{fig_HL-LHC_exclusion.pdf}
\caption{HL-LHC exclusion contours for BP1--BP4. The first two KK modes are
fully testable for all benchmark points.}
\label{fig:HL-LHC_exclusion}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Discovery Reach}

The discovery significance is
\begin{equation}
Z_{\text{disc}} = \sqrt{2\left[(S+B)\ln\left(1+\frac{S}{B}\right) - S\right]}.
\end{equation}

The HL-LHC can discover:
\begin{align}
m_1 &\lesssim 4.2~\text{TeV}, \\
m_2 &\lesssim 5.8~\text{TeV}.
\end{align}

\begin{figure}[h!]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\textwidth]{fig_discovery_reach.pdf}
\caption{HL-LHC discovery reach for BP1--BP4. The first KK mode is discoverable
for all benchmark points.}
\label{fig:discovery_reach}
\end{figure}

\subsection{FCC-hh Projections}

The FCC-hh at $\sqrt{s}=100~\text{TeV}$ dramatically extends reach. The projected
exclusion limits are:
\begin{align}
m_1 &\lesssim 12~\text{TeV}, \\
m_2 &\lesssim 18~\text{TeV}, \\
m_3 &\lesssim 25~\text{TeV}.
\end{align}

The discovery reach is:
\begin{align}
m_1 &\lesssim 10~\text{TeV}, \\
m_2 &\lesssim 15~\text{TeV}.
\end{align}

\begin{figure}[h!]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\textwidth]{fig_FCC-hh_reach.pdf}
\caption{FCC-hh exclusion and discovery reach for BP1--BP4. The full KK tower
is testable for all benchmark points.}
\label{fig:FCC-hh_reach}
\end{figure}

